# Docker-only node_modules

Never install or refresh Node dependencies on the host machine.

## Forbidden on the host

- `pnpm install` / `npm install` / `yarn install` (any variant, including `--frozen-lockfile`)
- Adding packages with `pnpm add` / `npm i` / `yarn add` that write into host `node_modules`
- `rm -rf node_modules` followed by a host reinstall
- Copying a host `node_modules` tree into a Docker image

## Required

- Install and run Node tooling **inside Docker** only (`Dockerfile` already runs `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`).
- Prefer `docker compose` for the app (`docker compose up --build`).
- For one-off scripts/tests/typecheck, run them in a container, e.g.:

```bash
docker compose run --rm --no-deps web pnpm test
docker compose run --rm --no-deps web pnpm typecheck
```

- If Compose is awkward for a one-shot, use `docker build` + `docker run --rm <image> pnpm <script>` instead of installing on the host.

## Why

Host `node_modules` drifts from the Linux image (optional native bindings, arch mismatches) and pollutes the working tree. The Dockerfile is the source of truth for dependency install.
